; 14-sandbox.kurt

; `sandbox` opens a scratch block: whatever you do inside is thrown away once
; the block closes, and never becomes part of the surrounding theory.  It's
; the place to try out a derivation without committing to it.

; A `sandbox` block can only be closed with `break` (25-break.kurt) -- not by
; `qed`, and not even by plain dedenting the way `assume`/`let`/`pick`/`proof`
; blocks are.  `break`, in turn, only works in the interactive shell.  So a
; `.kurt` *file* can open a `sandbox`, but cannot close it again -- which
; means this lesson is best read, and tried out live in the shell, rather
; than run as a file.  A transcript:
;
;     !!![1] bool A
;     !!![2] use A
;     !!![3] sandbox
;     sandbox                                    ; 3 open sandbox, close with `break`
;     !!![4]     use false
;         use false                              ; 4 without proof
;     !!![5]     false
;         false                                  ; 5 without proof
;     !!![6]     break
;     break                                       ; 6 forgot the last block
;     !!![7] A
;     A                                           ; 7 by 2
;     !!![8] false
;     !!!!!! ProofError: can not derive `false`
;
; Notice that `false`, freely available inside the sandbox, is gone again
; the moment `break` throws the block away -- while `A`, a real axiom from
; outside the sandbox, is still there.
;
; This particular file has no runnable `sandbox` block for the reason above;
; everything below is a normal top-level fact so the file still checks out.

bool A
use A
A
